ion_in_the_article":5,"Cell_line":"CHO","Time(h)":72,"IC50_Dark(M*10^-6)":"88.0 ± 3.0","IC50_Dark_value":88,"Method":"MTT","DOI":"10.1039/c9dt04269e","Year":2020,"Type":"IC50"}],"logp":[{"abbreviation_in_the_article":5,"value":-0.62,"value_relation":"=","type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"water","doi":"10.1039/c9dt04269e","year":2020,"record_id":"MLDB-002007","method_description":"The cellular accumulation of a chemotherapeutic agent is often correlated with its lipophilicity, so we studied the lipophilicity (log D) of 3–6 by the shake-flask method in an octanol/water mixture (Fig. 5b). The lowest log D was observed for 3, while 6showed the highest log D value. Complexes 3–5 were more hydrophilic in the order of 3 > 4 > 5, whereas 6 was moderately lipophilic. This lipophilicity pattern does not correlate with higher cellular accumulation or better cytotoxicity. The cytotoxicity is the highest for 5 (oxamusplatin) and also 5 accumulates the most in cells, but it is more hydrophilic than 6. Complexes 3and 5 are quite different in terms of lipophilicity, yet they are more similar in terms of accumulation in cells. Complex 6 is a stable complex with lipophilicity within the range for better accumulation, but it shows lower accumulation in cells."}]}